ber that this  agreement that's been broken is  happening at a time when ukraine and  russia are locked in a  profoundly bitter fratricidal war that  is still raging across the country so  to see um two ministers from the  respective countries sitting down albeit  separated by a fairly long table  is really something of an achievement  now it's taken to get this far nearly  three months of negotiations as you can  imagine there isn't much trust there's a  great deal of suspicion on both sides of  each other's  motives but at the end the agreement has  been signed now the goal in all of this  is of course it's not about ending the  war that is a i think a long way off but  it is about reintegrating  the ukrainian food economy back into  global markets now something like 22  million tons of grain is currently  trapped in silos  in ukrainian ports primarily the  ukrainian port city of odessa  and it can't can't get out because of a  naval blockade that russia has put in  place since it launched its invasion on  the 24th of february now this agreement  is fairly is very focused but what it  should mean in time and according to  senior un officials it could be as soon  as  10 days perhaps two weeks when we see  the first ships leaving with grain but  it should me should mean eventually and  to get fully working it perhaps might  take a month because it is a big  logistical challenge but you could see  something like five million metric tons  of grain leaving ukrainian ports heading  through the black sea and uh into  turkish waters through the bosphorus  where it will be distributed around the  world particularly to countries in the  global south which are really facing  inflationary pressure as a result of the  cost of living crisis and the specter of  hunger and famine is becoming more and  more real every day now according to the  world food programme something like 47  million people are facing acute hunger  as a direct  consequence of this conflict and 71  million p